---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { latestVersion } from "../lib/version";

// Resolved at build time so the install pin cannot go stale in the page.
const version = await latestVersion();
---

<Base
  title="Quick start"
  description="Install PolicyForge, the open-source tool, set up a project, and draft your first Standard in about ninety seconds."
>
  <main>
    <section class="page-hero">
      <div class="container">
        <p class="eyebrow">Quick start</p>
        <h1>Draft your first Standard in about ninety seconds.</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          PolicyForge is the open-source tool behind this site. You install it as the
          <code>policyforge</code> command. Six steps, two AI calls, and on a budget model
          less than a cent.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="container">
        <ol class="qs" style="list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0;">
          <li class="qs-step">
            <span class="qs-n">1</span>
            <div class="qs-body">
              <h3>Install</h3>
              <pre><code>brew install rdazzleman/tap/policyforge

<span class="cmt"># or, without Homebrew:</span>
pipx install git+https://github.com/rdazzleman/policyforge@{version}</code></pre>
              <p class="muted small">
                The Homebrew version includes the common AI providers. Amazon Bedrock, Google
                Vertex, LiteLLM and the MCP connector for AI assistants are optional extras,
                available through pipx.
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>

          <li class="qs-step">
            <span class="qs-n">2</span>
            <div class="qs-body">
              <h3>Create a project</h3>
              <p>This makes a folder with the included frameworks and example settings.</p>
              <pre><code>policyforge init my-policies && cd my-policies</code></pre>
            </div>
          </li>

          <li class="qs-step">
            <span class="qs-n">3</span>
            <div class="qs-body">
              <h3>Connect your AI model</h3>
              <p>
                Copy the example settings, then set your API key. The settings file stores
                the <em>name</em> of the variable holding your key, never the key itself.
              </p>
              <pre><code>cp config/config.example.yaml config/config.yaml
cp config/topics.example.yaml config/topics.yaml

export ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-...   <span class="cmt"># whichever variable your settings name</span>
policyforge llm-check             <span class="cmt"># confirms the key and model work</span></code></pre>
              <div class="callout">
                <p>
                  <strong>The topic list is yours to edit.</strong> It starts with twenty
                  common topics, each with an owning team. It is really a statement of how
                  your organization is arranged, so adjust it to match.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </li>

          <li class="qs-step">
            <span class="qs-n">4</span>
            <div class="qs-body">
              <h3>Map the frameworks together</h3>
              <p>No AI is used here, so this step is free.</p>
              <pre><code>policyforge map \
  --controls data/frameworks/nist-800-53-r5/controls.json \
  --controls data/frameworks/hipaa-security-rule/controls.json \
  --controls data/frameworks/fedramp/controls.json \
  --controls data/frameworks/arc-ampe/controls.json</code></pre>
            </div>
          </li>

          <li class="qs-step">
            <span class="qs-n">5</span>
            <div class="qs-body">
              <h3>Merge one topic’s requirements</h3>
              <p>The first AI call. This one list feeds all three documents for the topic.</p>
              <pre><code>policyforge synthesize \
  --topic-name "Identity Lifecycle & Access Review" \
  --controls data/frameworks/nist-800-53-r5/controls.json \
  --controls data/frameworks/hipaa-security-rule/controls.json</code></pre>
            </div>
          </li>

          <li class="qs-step">
            <span class="qs-n">6</span>
            <div class="qs-body">
              <h3>Write the Standard</h3>
              <pre><code>policyforge generate --tier standard \
  --synthesis output/synthesis/identity-lifecycle-access-review.md</code></pre>
            </div>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section sunk">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="grid-2">
          <div class="card">
            <h3>What you get</h3>
            <p>
              The Standard in <code>output/standards/</code>, the merged requirements in
              <code>output/synthesis/</code>, saved versions in <code>output/.history/</code>,
              and a record of every AI call from <code>policyforge model-log</code>.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <h3>Expect square brackets</h3>
            <p>
              The draft will name things like <code>[Identity Provider]</code> and
              <code>[organization-defined frequency]</code>. Those are decisions only you can
              make, left visible on purpose. Fill in tools and teams in
              <code>config/config.yaml</code> and values with <code>policyforge parameters</code>,
              then generate again.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <h3>Where to go next</h3>
            <p>
              Write the Policy and Procedure with <code>--tier policy</code> and
              <code>--tier procedure</code>. Run <code>policyforge coverage</code> to find
              controls no topic owns. Then run <code>policyforge zardoz</code> and start asking
              questions.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <h3>Before you rely on it</h3>
            <p>
              Every draft needs a qualified person to read, correct and own it. PolicyForge
              writes documents; it does not make you compliant or produce audit evidence.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</Base>
